  EML Stats : Normality Assessment
  Fri Aug 14 02:54:23 2026
══════════════════════════════════════════════

  Table               voiceA
  Column              spl
  N                   24


  ── Descriptive Statistics ──────────────────
  Mean                64.8072
  SD                  3.2356
  Median              64.5422


  ── Distribution Shape ──────────────────────
  Why: Skewness and kurtosis indicate departures from a normal (bell-shaped) distribution.
  Skewness            0.1775		Approximately symmetric (|skew| < 2 is typically acceptable)
  Kurtosis (excess)   -1.0820		Near-normal peakedness (0 = normal; |excess| < 7 treated as typical)
  → Skewness within typical limits (criterion: |skew| < 2)
  → Kurtosis within typical limits (criterion: |excess kurt| < 7)


  ── Shapiro-Wilk Test ───────────────────────
  Why: Formal test of whether the data are drawn from a normal distribution.
  W                   0.9502		Closer to 1 = more normal (range: 0 to 1)
  p                   .274		Not statistically significant at the 5% level
  → Does not reject normality (p >= 0.05)


  ── Recommendation ──────────────────────────
  Normality appears reasonable.
  → Parametric tests (t-test, ANOVA, Pearson r) are appropriate.

  Conventions: quartiles R type 7 · SD & variance n-1
  · rank tests average tied ranks (tie-corrected).
